Looking at this code, I see the borrow-checker error. The issue is that `longest` is trying to hold a reference to an element in the `items` vector, but when we iterate over `items` with `for item in items`, we're taking ownership of each element (consuming the vector). This creates a problem because:

1. We're trying to store a reference to an element that might be moved out
2. The loop variable `item` is owned, so `&item` creates a reference to a local variable that goes out of scope

The fix is to iterate by reference instead of by value, so we don't consume the vector and can safely take references to its elements.

```rust
fn longest_len(items: Vec<String>) -> usize {
    if items.is_empty() {
        return 0;
    }

    let mut longest = &items[0];
    for item in &items {
        if item.len() > longest.len() {
            longest = item;
        }
    }
    longest.len()
}

fn main() {
    let v = vec![String::from("a"), String::from("bb"), String::from("ccc")];
    println!("{}", longest_len(v));
}
```

